---
// A real review: CodeMop's inline comment and summary on its own pull request #20, as posted
// (github.com/sgtwickool/codemop/pull/20). The boxes work: tick the fix, then "Commit the
// ticked fixes", and the summary shows what happened on the PR.
---

<div class="demo not-content">
  <article class="comment">
    <header>
      <span class="avatar" aria-hidden="true"></span>
      <strong>CodeMop</strong> commented on <code>src/codemop/cli/post.py</code>, lines 143–144
    </header>
    <div class="body">
      <p class="title">🐛 Bug: Title fallback can resolve another finding's conversation</p>
      <p>
        <code>by_title</code> is built from every CodeMop thread, including ones that carry a finding id. Suppose an
        addressed finding has no inline comment (it was over <code>max_comments</code>), and a different, still-open
        finding with the same path and title has a thread. That thread gets resolved, and the next
        <code>sync_threads</code> then dismisses the open finding. The path/title fallback should only use threads from
        before comments carried ids.
      </p>
      <div class="suggestion">
        <div class="suggestion-head">Suggested change</div>
        <pre><span class="del">-    by_title = &#123;(t.path, c.title): t for c, t in conversations(threads)&#125;</span>
<span class="add">+    by_title = &#123;(t.path, c.title): t for c, t in conversations(threads) if c.finding_id is None&#125;</span>
<span>     to_resolve = [by_finding.get(f.id) or by_title.get((f.path, f.title)) for f in addressed]</span></pre>
      </div>
      <p class="footer">
        CodeMop · confidence 0.50 · Not a problem? Resolve this conversation and CodeMop won't raise it again on this
        PR, or reply <code>/codemop learn &lt;why&gt;</code> to teach it for the whole repository.
      </p>
    </div>
  </article>

  <article class="comment">
    <header>
      <span class="avatar" aria-hidden="true"></span>
      <strong>CodeMop</strong> commented
    </header>
    <div class="body">
      <p class="heading">CodeMop review of 7f67de8</p>
      <p>
        1 open issue(s). Tick the fixes you want, then tick <strong>Commit the ticked fixes</strong> at the end, and
        CodeMop commits them to this branch as one commit.
      </p>
      <ul class="checklist">
        <li>
          <label>
            <input type="checkbox" data-fix />
            <span>🐛 Bug <code>src/codemop/cli/post.py:143-144</code>: Title fallback can resolve another finding's
              conversation<span class="applied" hidden> · ✅ applied in 7978a89</span></span>
          </label>
        </li>
        <li>
          <label>
            <input type="checkbox" data-commit />
            <strong>Commit the ticked fixes</strong>
          </label>
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p class="status" aria-live="polite"></p>
      <p class="footer">anthropic/claude-opus-5-5 · 63,417 input / 5,327 output tokens · about $0.36</p>
    </div>
  </article>
</div>

<script>
  for (const demo of document.querySelectorAll<HTMLElement>(".demo")) {
    const fix = demo.querySelector<HTMLInputElement>("[data-fix]")!;
    const commit = demo.querySelector<HTMLInputElement>("[data-commit]")!;
    const applied = demo.querySelector<HTMLElement>(".applied")!;
    const status = demo.querySelector<HTMLElement>(".status")!;
    commit.addEventListener("change", () => {
      if (!commit.checked) return;
      if (!fix.checked) {
        status.textContent = "No ticked fixes to commit yet: tick the fix first.";
      } else {
        status.textContent = "Committed 7978a89 to the PR's branch.";
        applied.hidden = false;
        fix.disabled = true;
      }
      // As on GitHub, the box unticks itself, ready for next time
      setTimeout(() => (commit.checked = false), 600);
    });
  }
</script>

<style>
